+\subsection{\newrev{Baselines, Ablation Variants, and Metrics}}
 \label{subsec:baselines_metrics}
-We compare HARP with heuristic, learning-based, and runtime variants following recent DRL, GCN-based, and migration-aware SFC orchestration studies~\cite{xiao2023twostage,liu2024sfcml,addad2022ai,tariq2023fipam,afrasiabi2024cluster}. \textbf{Greedy} is a short-horizon placement--routing baseline that uses local feasibility and immediate cost, but does not account for future fragmentation, SID-stack pressure, or migration effects. \textbf{Decoupled AI (DAI)} uses learned placement or routing behavior without the joint graph-aware masking structure used by HARP. \textbf{SRv6-aware feasible heuristic (SAF-H)} filters candidate placements and paths by CPU, memory, and SRv6 MSD feasibility before admission, then selects the feasible action with the lowest weighted cost. SAF-H is a stronger deterministic baseline than Greedy, but remains myopic and non-adaptive. In the hardware-in-the-loop testbed, we additionally evaluate a safety-protected \textbf{Heuristic runtime} and a \textbf{Hybrid runtime}; the latter uses the heuristic path under stable load and invokes HARP under stress, alert, or migration pressure.
-
-We use two comparison modes. In the simulation study, Greedy, DAI, SAF-H, and HARP are evaluated under the same topology, workload generator, resource-capacity settings, MSD profile, and metric definitions, so that safe acceptance and pre-admission infeasible pressure are measured consistently. In the hardware-in-the-loop testbed, HARP, Hybrid runtime, and Heuristic runtime are evaluated as safety-protected runtime variants because their decisions pass through the AIO-Mesh admission boundary before actuation. Greedy and DAI are evaluated as unprotected stress baselines; their role is to expose the data-plane consequence of bypassing hardware-safe admission checks, rather than to represent protected AIO-Mesh runtime behavior.
-
-For ablation, \textbf{PPO-MLP} replaces the graph-attention encoder with a flat MLP. \textbf{HARP w/o masking} disables policy-side hard masking while retaining runtime admission. \textbf{HARP w/o mask + unchecked} removes both policy-side masking and runtime admission checks, and is used only as a diagnostic unsafe variant. \textbf{HARP-SoftMSD} treats MSD feasibility as a reward penalty. \textbf{HARP w/o adaptive penalty} uses fixed soft-constraint weights, and \textbf{HARP w/o migration} disables proactive migration actions. These variants isolate topology awareness, hard deployability enforcement, runtime safety validation, adaptive soft-constraint control, and migration behavior.
-
-We report acceptance rate, safe acceptance rate, pre-admission hard-infeasible candidate rate, admitted MSD violation rate, average latency, SLA violation rate, queueing delay, and reward. Safe acceptance is the primary admission metric because it counts only requests satisfying compute, memory, and MSD feasibility. Pre-admission hard-infeasible candidate rate measures how strongly the workload pushes the system outside the feasible region before admission, while admitted MSD violation rate measures actual data-plane safety failure after admission.
-
