+\newrev{Decoupled AI is the benchmark name for a sequential CPU/RAM heuristic. SAF-H searches feasible placement--routing pairs using the cost defined above. The current benchmark evaluates the corrected flat N²-action HARP retrains, while ablation evaluates its existing separately trained flat-action variants. Archived factorized-head benchmark outputs are historical and are not pooled with the new N² runs.}
+
+\newrev{The flat-action ablation defines Full with a graph encoder, action mask, and adaptive SLA penalty. MLP changes the encoder, NoMask removes policy masking, and NoAdapt fixes the SLA coefficient. SoftMSD uses an MSD reward penalty without a policy mask. NoMask and SoftMSD each have an evaluation mode with the MSD admission check disabled, while other resource checks stay active. Each bypass uses its corresponding checkpoint.}
+
+\newrev{The refreshed algorithm benchmark reports safe acceptance per offered, non-skipped request and aggregates 20 seed-level rates with sample standard deviation (SD). For each protected HARP--SAF-H and HARP--CSPF contrast, paired seed-level acceptance differences are tested using the two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test~\cite{wilcoxon1945}; percentile-bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals (CIs) use 20,000 paired resamples, and Holm correction~\cite{holm1979} is applied separately across the nine topology--workload comparisons in each family. The paired rank-biserial effect is positive when HARP tends to have higher acceptance; it describes the signed rank balance, not the magnitude in percentage points. The timing-instrumented ablation reports safe acceptance and policy-decision P95 over 20 evaluation seeds using mean and sample SD. The matched-trace ablation reports sample SD across seed--workload evaluation cells. These deviations describe evaluation variation conditional on the evaluated checkpoints, not variation across independently trained policies.}
+
+\newrev{For the timing-instrumented and matched-trace ablations, safe acceptance is accepted requests minus the unique admitted requests violating any modeled hard CPU, memory, bandwidth, or MSD constraint, divided by offered non-skipped requests. Proposed CPU, memory, bandwidth, and MSD violation rates may overlap; the union rate is reported separately. Candidate pruning is computed over the flat $N^2$ action set.}
+
+\newrev{For the primary stress contrasts, the 21 offered-intensity HARP--SAF-H contrasts, the separate 15 fixed-profile arrival-rate contrasts, the strongest-baseline follow-up, sparse-adjacency comparisons, and TTL/mask intervention, the signed-rank calculation drops exactly zero paired differences, assigns average ranks to tied absolute differences, and computes the two-sided conditional sign-flip distribution by exact enumeration of rank sums. All-zero contrasts have \(p=1\) and remain in the applicable Holm family; a rank-biserial effect is not informative for such a contrast. Bootstrap intervals are pointwise and are not adjusted for multiplicity. Holm adjustment applies to the stated p-value family, not to these intervals.}
+
+\newrev{All simulation rates use offered requests as the denominator unless stated otherwise. The simulator marks an arrival-free step as skipped. The stress benchmark and matched-trace ablation use arrival probability 1.0, so every seed contributes 3,000 requests. At lower intensities there can be fewer offered requests than steps. Runtime rejection and admitted violation are separate outcomes; neither is a measure of successful physical deployment. Algorithm-benchmark comparisons pair policies by topology, workload, and seed and verify identical offered-trace hashes. The matched-trace ablation likewise reuses the offered trace across variants, although policy-dependent admissions can cause simulated state trajectories to diverge.}
+
