-The ablation confirms four points. First, replacing graph attention with a flat MLP reduces safe acceptance and increases latency, showing that topology information is important for placement--routing decisions. Second, disabling policy-side masking while keeping runtime admission can remain safe on the small Vietnam topology, but it loses admission quality under bursty load; the unchecked variant exposes the true risk by producing \(23.22\%{-}31.78\%\) admitted MSD violations. Third, treating MSD as a reward penalty is ineffective: SoftMSD collapses safe acceptance to about \(1\%{-}2\%\) and still admits many MSD-violating requests. Fourth, removing adaptive penalties remains safe but reduces safe acceptance, indicating that adaptive soft-constraint control improves service quality without weakening hardware safety.
