+\newrev{Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:ablation_pruning} compares candidate pruning and safe acceptance. Candidate pruning is the per-step fraction of the flat $N^2$ action set removed before policy sampling. Runtime rejection and safe acceptance use offered, non-skipped requests as the denominator; admitted hard violations count the union of the modeled CPU, RAM, bandwidth, and MSD violations among admitted requests, divided by all offered requests. The full policy prunes 74.72--84.20\% of candidates across the three topologies, including decision states with an empty mask. Table~\ref{tab:mask_admission} separates empty-mask frequency and conditional pruning. Empty masks remove every candidate. With equal-length runs, pruning fraction \(p\) and empty-mask fraction \(e\) give conditional pruning \((p-e)/(1-e)\). Empty-mask frequency and rejection rate are marginal summaries. They do not establish how many proposals from nonempty masks were rejected. The pooled ablation comprises 720,000 simulator steps per variant. Each step in this protocol offers one request. On this evaluation, the no-mask variant proposed a hard-invalid action on 75.17\% of offered requests, compared with 41.64\% for the full policy; the admission check rejected these proposals, and no hard-invalid requests were admitted in either protected condition. Because the policies differ as well as the masks, this comparison does not isolate the causal effect of masking. Per-constraint proposal rates in Table~\ref{tab:proposal_violations} overlap; their union avoids double-counting requests. The MSD reward coefficient is $-50$. With the same NoMask checkpoint, disabling MSD admission admitted 9 MSD-invalid actions over the 720,000-step evaluation (0.00125\%); the SoftMSD checkpoint admitted 182 such actions (0.02528\%) with MSD admission disabled and none with it enabled. CPU, RAM, and bandwidth checks remained active in both MSD-admission-bypass conditions. Although paired variants receive the same offered trace, different admission decisions can change later states, so request outcomes are not per-request counterfactual pairs. The reported latency field measures simulated service latency, not policy-computation overhead. These results characterize simulator predicates; forwarding on the BMv2 testbed is reported separately in Section~\ref{subsec:testbed_validation}.}
