+\newrev{\textbf{End-to-end campaigns.} The campaign driver submitted requests with the alert flag set, so the Hybrid DRL branch could request deployment or migration. Requested CPU, RAM, and SID depth (2--5) were drawn by a seeded generator, and the corrected campaign stopped when 30 active VNF names were absent from its starting baseline. This name-set target counts replacements of baseline VNFs and is not a target of 30 net additions. Each admitted request kept one identifier from admission to packet observation. For each request we logged the HTTP 200 response with the admitted SID list and switch path, the deployment phase and Tekton PipelineRun, Pod readiness, the controller's rule-installation log, a readback of every expected table entry on each switch of the path, the controller's steering confirmation for that request, and a 20-packet probe. The probe source applied Linux SRv6 encapsulation with the admitted SID list; BMv2 advanced the segments, the placement switch decapsulated the packet with the End.DX4 behaviour (decapsulation and IPv4 cross-connect), and the VNF Pod answered (an HTTP marker for vVOC and an Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP) echo payload derived from the request identifier otherwise). Table~\ref{tab:hil_e2e} reports the campaign with the corrected checkpoint used in the simulator evaluation and an earlier campaign with the pre-correction checkpoint. The two campaigns started from different testbed states and are not paired.}
+
+\newrev{With the corrected checkpoint, all \HILEtoESteered{} admitted requests reached steering confirmation, and readback found every expected rule on every path switch. \HILEtoEFullProbes{} probes delivered all 20 packets. The five remaining probes targeted Pods on the Kubernetes control-plane node before its firewall permitted Calico Virtual Extensible LAN (VXLAN) traffic on UDP port 4789 and forwarding from the VXLAN interface. After this network correction, re-probes delivered 20/20 packets to all four VNFs still deployed; the fifth had been replaced by a later migration. Component percentiles are computed separately and must not be summed. Most rejections (231) occurred because the requested SID depth differed from the depth of the materialized shortest path; six occurred because the materialized path exceeded a switch MSD profile, and no rule was installed for them. The remaining rejections were 43 proposals with identical ingress and placement and 49 with the explicit log message \texttt{resilience\_no\_safe\_pair\_available}. These four rejection categories sum to 329; with 69 admissions they account for all 398 offered requests. The campaign began with 39 active VNFs. The 69 admissions comprised 12 deployments without replacement and 57 migrations, yielding \(39+12+57-57=51\) active VNFs. At termination, 21 baseline names remained and 30 active names were absent from the baseline; hence the driver's target was met despite a net increase of only 12. Of the admitted requests, \HILEtoEMigrations{} replaced an existing VNF. In every migration the new VNF was Ready before steering confirmation, and confirmation preceded the deletion request for the old VNF. Clients address the Pod IP, which changes on migration, and flow continuity across migration was not measured; these data do not support a zero-downtime claim.}
