+\newrev{In the refreshed offered-intensity evaluation, HARP's safe-acceptance advantage over SAF-H at intensity 1.2 was 2.515, 3.213, and 3.680 percentage points on Vietnam, NSFNET, and GEANT2, respectively. At intensity 1.5, the differences were 2.402, 2.697, and 3.303 points. The paired seed-level confidence intervals and corrected tests are reported in the refreshed evidence package. These estimates are conditional on fixed N² checkpoints and the offered workload, not independently trained policy populations.}
+
+\newrev{Acceptance is substantially higher under the shorter, topology-scaled TTL protocol than in the three-workload stress benchmark. The favorable sweep results therefore identify a workload regime in which HARP improves safe acceptance relative to SAF-H; they do not establish an advantage under long-lived stress traffic or explain individual rejections as globally infeasible.}
+
+\newrev{To quantify rejected choices with feasible alternatives, we exhaustively evaluated the simulator's complete 10-node pair-action space at sampled decision states. For each of 10 seeds, 120 states were sampled at a stride of 25 from a 3,000-step Bursty run. Every one of the 100 placement--routing pairs was replayed from the same pre-decision snapshot through the simulator step, including its TTL-expiration phase before admission. In the refreshed Vietnam run, HARP selected a rejected action at 583/1,200 sampled states; 30 had an alternative accepted by the same simulator predicate. The per-seed rate of such alternatives among HARP's selected rejections averaged 5.16\% (sample SD 1.84\%). SAF-H had 579/1,200 selected rejections, of which 33 had an accepted alternative; the corresponding mean was 5.70\% (sample SD 2.32\%). These descriptive rates do not use sampled decisions as independent replicates; policy trajectories also differ. They identify avoidable choices in the simulator's pair space, not live-network or full-chain feasibility.}
