+\subsection{\newrev{Operational Interpretation, Overhead, and Limitations}}
+\label{subsec:operational_interpretation}
+\newrev{The policy comparison depends on the resource-pressure regime. SAF-H achieves higher acceptance in all six Vietnam and NSFNET stress conditions, and in GEANT2 Chaos; HARP improves GEANT2 Burst-surge acceptance by 0.808 percentage points, while the Elephant-stress difference is inconclusive. Under the shorter-lifetime load-sweep protocol, the refreshed model comparisons show higher HARP mean safe acceptance than SAF-H at intensities 1.0--1.5 on all three substrates. The background-occupancy experiment also favors HARP within its protocol. Together, these findings support workload-specific policy selection within a common admission interface.}
+
+\newrev{Acceptance gains must also be weighed against computation. In the Vietnam ablation, the MLP variant achieves slightly higher mean safe acceptance and lower mean seed-level decision P95 than the full GAT policy under both Uniform and Bursty traffic (Figure~\ref{fig:ablation_cost}). In the controlled HARP--SAF-H comparison, HARP's mean decision P95 was 4.3 times SAF-H's, while the HARP policy-inference component alone averaged 1.617~ms at P95. Thus the load-sweep acceptance gains have a measurable local computation cost, and the evidence supports a workload-dependent acceptance--cost tradeoff rather than a general efficiency advantage. The common admission boundary permits either policy to be selected without assigning the enforcement outcome to that policy.}
+
+\newrev{Appendix Table~\ref{tab:stress_qos} reports service latency and SLA outcomes for HARP and SAF-H by workload. Across the nine stress conditions, mean seed-level simulated service latency is 5.25--7.39~ms for HARP and 0.32--0.62~ms for SAF-H; mean seed-level SLA-violation rates are approximately 0.00--0.21\% and zero, respectively. These quantities describe the requests admitted by each policy, whose composition may differ. SAF-H may choose the same node for placement and routing. HARP also permits that pair but its reward includes a bonus for distinct endpoints. They provide no evidence of a HARP service-latency advantage in these conditions and are distinct from policy-computation timing.}
+
+\newrev{Simulation uses complete latency-derived graphs and synthetic workloads. The primary policy comparisons use fixed checkpoints. Supplementary Table~S22 reports three independently trained Vietnam models on one workload; it does not establish training variability across all evaluated topologies and workloads. The primary load and occupancy studies reuse one Bursty offered trace; the supplementary seed-offset intervention covers trace variation. MSD violations were infrequent among simulator proposals. The multilayer perceptron (MLP) was sufficient for the evaluated Vietnam ablations, and graph attention may behave differently on sparse graphs. Synthetic-grid results include HARP and SAF-H; timing from concurrent benchmark runs is not isolated. The historical serialized SAF-H tail and the concurrent HARP--SAF-H benchmark are distinct studies; neither identifies shared-host scheduling as the cause of a particular tail. The testbed uses BMv2 software switches, so its forwarding results do not transfer to hardware ASICs. The end-to-end campaigns cover one testbed and one seeded request generator, whose requested SID depths often differed from the materialized path depth. The network correction on the Kubernetes control-plane node was runtime-only, and the s10 readback source correction was not installed in the live controller during these runs. Migration changes the Pod IP and flow continuity was not measured, so make-before-break ordering is shown but zero downtime is not. Transitions between Hybrid modes remain a separate evaluation question.}
+
+\section{Conclusion}
+\label{sec:conclusion}
+This paper presented \textbf{AIO-Mesh}, a hardware-safe AIOps orchestration framework for cloud-native service function chains over programmable SRv6/P4 data planes. The central motivation is that learned orchestration policies cannot be treated as offline optimizers when their outputs directly affect service placement, traffic steering, migration, and programmable forwarding behavior. In such environments, a logically efficient service path may still be non-deployable if its SID stack exceeds the parser-depth capability of the forwarding profile. \newrev{The common admission boundary rejected modeled hard-invalid proposals from learned and heuristic policies. On BMv2, the retained campaigns linked admission to deployment, rule readback, steering confirmation, and packet observations at individual VNFs. Controlled over-depth injection produced drops at the expected switch.}
+
+\newrev{Policy effectiveness was workload dependent: SAF-H generally led in long-lived stress, while HARP achieved higher mean acceptance in the evaluated high-intensity and occupancy protocols at greater decision cost.} The current prototype has several limitations. \newrev{The evaluated data plane uses BMv2 software switches; the cited campaign records do not by themselves establish ordered multi-VNF traversal or migration flow continuity. The multi-hour runs measure admission and probes to previously steered VNFs, rather than repeated admission-to-actuation lifecycle stability.}
+
